  b. Then locate the scheme delimiter by a plain first-occurrence find of the 3-byte sequence "://" anywhere in what remains, with NO validation of the preceding bytes (they may contain '/', ':' , spaces, anything): 'a/b://c/d' -> scheme "a/b", host "c", path "/d" -> 1 ["d"]. Only the first "://" is consumed; later ones are ordinary path bytes: 'http://e.com/a/http://b/c' -> 4 ["a","http:","b","c"].
  c. If "://" is absent, the whole (already truncated) string is the path: 'example.com' -> 1; ':' -> 1 [":"]; 'a/b/c' -> 3.
  d. If "://" is present, the authority runs from just after it up to the FIRST 0x2F byte, and the path is that '/' and everything after it (empty if there is no such '/'). The authority scan is a bare find('/') — it is NOT bracket-aware, NOT '@'-aware and NOT ':'-aware, so a '/' inside brackets, userinfo or the port field ends the authority: 'http://[::1/x]/y' -> path "/x]/y" -> 2 ["x]","y"]; 'http://[/a/b/c]' -> 3 ["a","b","c]"]; 'http://u/p@e.com/a' -> 2 ["p@e.com","a"]; 'http://e.com:8/0/a' -> port 8 valid, path "/0/a" -> 2 ["0","a"]. Extra slashes right after "://" simply produce an empty host and are then collapsed by split_path: 'http:///a/b', 'http:////a/b', 'http://///a' -> 2, 2, 1.
  e. No whole-string trim: '  http://e.com/a  ' -> path "/a  " -> 1 segment "a  " (leading/trailing tabs and newlines likewise preserved, od -c verified).

Remaining genuinely unobservable through this driver (not defects, just unreachable): an embedded NUL (0x00) cannot be delivered via argv, so its status as an ordinary segment byte is inferred, not observed; and whether the implementation uses find('/')+substr or getline(ss,item,'/') is behaviourally indistinguishable (both match every probe, including the trailing-separator cases where getline yields no final empty piece, because empty pieces are dropped either way). One driver-level (not split_path) caveat the original rule omitted: a segment containing 0x0A is printed with its newline intact, so LINES 6+ are ambiguous unless you rely on the LINE 5 count (e.g. 'http://e.com/A\nB/z' -> count 2, segments "A\nB" and "z").
